tools.description:
Create or safely reuse a complete multi-layer variable foundation in one call. The handler validates request structure before writing, then creates collections, primitive variables, and semantic aliases in dependency order while resolving local keys to real Figma IDs. Conflicts discovered against existing Figma content stop the operation. Later failures trigger best-effort rollback of newly created artifacts; any retained IDs or rollback errors are reported explicitly.

Use only when the user supplied all of the following:

- every collection name and complete ordered mode list;
- every primitive variable name, type, collection, and value for every mode in that collection;
- every semantic alias name, type, collection, and primitive target for every mode in that collection.

One call accepts at most 10 collections and 50 total primitives plus aliases. Split a larger foundation into independent complete calls.

`key` and `collection` are local references within this call, not Figma IDs. `values_by_mode` and `targets_by_mode` keys must exactly match the modes of their referenced collection. Every alias target must be a primitive key in this call with the same type. A same-name collection with incompatible modes is rejected instead of creating a duplicate.

Do not use for binding existing nodes, switching modes, adding or renaming a mode on an existing collection, partial token specifications, or aliases that target pre-existing variables. Use the focused variable tools for those operations.

Example:

```json
{
  "collections": [
    { "key": "raw", "name": "Primitives", "modes": ["Value"] },
    { "key": "semantic", "name": "Semantic", "modes": ["Light", "Dark"] }
  ],
  "primitives": [
    {
      "key": "brand-blue",
      "collection": "raw",
      "name": "Brand/Blue",
      "type": "COLOR",
      "values_by_mode": { "Value": "#335CFF" }
    },
    {
      "key": "space-8",
      "collection": "raw",
      "name": "Space/8",
      "type": "FLOAT",
      "values_by_mode": { "Value": 8 }
    }
  ],
  "aliases": [
    {
      "key": "color-action",
      "collection": "semantic",
      "name": "Color/Action",
      "type": "COLOR",
      "targets_by_mode": { "Light": "brand-blue", "Dark": "brand-blue" }
    },
    {
      "key": "space-control",
      "collection": "semantic",
      "name": "Space/Control",
      "type": "FLOAT",
      "targets_by_mode": { "Light": "space-8", "Dark": "space-8" }
    }
  ]
}
```

Combine 2+ existing COMPONENTs into a COMPONENT_SET (variant set). The new set takes over the layout slot of the first input's parent; the input components become its children (the variants). Sensible defaults applied: horizontal wrap layout, 24px gap, 20px padding.

Use this tool to create variant axes such as Size, State, or Theme. Figma derives axes from component names in `Axis=Value` form. Pass `variants` with the same axis keys for every component, and the tool will name them correctly. Omit `variants` only when the component names already use `Axis=Value`; otherwise Figma creates one untyped axis named `Property 1`.

To build a variant set: create one COMPONENT per combination (create_component on frames, or jsx), then combine_components with `variants`.

Use when:
- You have ≥2 COMPONENTs that vary along one or more axes (Size, State, Theme) and need a typed variant set
- For per-instance label, icon, visibility, or slot changes, use `add_component_prop` instead of a variant axis

Returns: { data: { message, nodeId: "1:5", variants: ["Size=Small", "Size=Large"] } }

Skip when:
- Inputs are FRAMEs — run create_component on each first
- The variation is per-instance rather than per-component shape — use add_component_prop

Examples:
  // Single axis (Size), handler names the children:
```text
combine_components({nodes: ["1:2", "1:3", "1:4"], name: "Button", variants: [
  {node: "1:2", props: {Size: "Small"}},
  {node: "1:3", props: {Size: "Medium"}},
  {node: "1:4", props: {Size: "Large"}}
]})
// Two axes (Size × State):
combine_components({nodes: ["1:2", "1:3"], name: "Button", variants: [
  {node: "1:2", props: {Size: "Small", State: "Default"}},
  {node: "1:3", props: {Size: "Small", State: "Hover"}}
]})
// Components already named "Axis=Value" — no mapping needed:
combine_components({nodes: ["1:2", "1:3"], name: "Button"})
```
